"Shows locations reachable from or toward a specified place within defined travel-time thresholds under stated travel assumptions.\n\nUse when: Compare service accessibility or candidate facility catchments. Explain how network conditions change realistic reach beyond simple distance circles.\n\nAvoid when: The audience expects a guaranteed travel time or the model lacks a valid travel network.\n\nVisual grammar: Nested boundaries or filled bands encode travel-time thresholds. Origin markers distinguish the analyzed starting or destination locations. Legend notes identify mode, direction, departure time and network assumptions.\n\nBuild: Choose origins, travel direction, mode and relevant time conditions. Calculate reach using the applicable network and restrictions. Display meaningful thresholds and disclose unreachable gaps and model limitations.\n\nCheck: Threshold units and time assumptions are explicit. Toward and away-from-origin analyses are not conflated. Road, barrier and mode restrictions match the intended scenario.\n\nPitfalls: Substituting straight-line radius for modeled travel time. Treating generalized catchment polygons as a promise for every address.\n\nExample: A community center compares walking reach within two time thresholds, accounting for river crossings and separating disconnected inaccessible areas.\n\nAlternatives: Isoline map, Reference map."
